 # Densification Strategy
-Also called: Dynamic Primitive Management, Adaptive Density Control, Densification and Splitting
+
+Also called: Adaptive Density Control, Dynamic Topology Management, Primitive Lifecycle Management
 
 ## Overview
-Densification strategies dynamically manage the number, distribution, and attributes of 3D Gaussian primitives during the training of 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) [Parent]. They address the problem of under-representing complex scene details or over-representing empty space by splitting, cloning, or pruning Gaussians based on optimization metrics [Parent]. What sets this family apart from static initialization methods is the iterative, feedback-driven adaptation of the primitive set throughout the training process, balancing reconstruction quality against computational cost [Parent].
+Densification strategies manage the dynamic topology of 3D Gaussian primitives during training to balance reconstruction fidelity against computational cost. They solve the problem of under-representation (blurry regions) and over-representation (redundant primitives or "floaters") by periodically splitting, cloning, or pruning Gaussians based on optimization metrics. What sets this family apart from static initialization methods is the active modification of the primitive set during the optimization loop, and from pure pruning strategies by including constructive operations (splitting/cloning) to increase density.
 
