 ## Background
-The concept of densification in 3D Gaussian Splatting originates from the original 3DGS paper [Parent], which introduced the heuristic of splitting and cloning Gaussians based on 2D positional gradients to handle under-reconstructed regions. Subsequent developments have diversified this approach to address specific challenges such as dynamic scenes ([GroupFlow](6351-groupflow.md)), memory efficiency ([Locality-preserving Densification](9585-locality-preserving-densification.md)), and robustness to sparse views ([Multi-view Alpha-based Densification and Pruning](15406-multi-view-alpha-based-densification-and-pruning.md)). The family has expanded to include MCMC-based sampling ([3DGS-MCMC](7852-3dgs-mcmc.md)) and neural refinement ([Adaptive Gaussian Refinement](11448-adaptive-gaussian-refinement.md)), reflecting the ongoing effort to optimize the trade-off between representation fidelity and computational cost.
+The concept of adaptive densification was introduced in the original 3D Gaussian Splatting paper [4] (cited in [S1]) to handle the sparse initial point clouds from Structure-from-Motion (SfM). The original method used 2D positional gradient magnitudes to trigger splitting and cloning. Subsequent research has expanded this family to include MCMC-based approaches [S1][S2, unofficial], multi-view consistency metrics [S1], and memory-aware optimizations [S1]. The family also includes variants for dynamic scenes [S1] and feed-forward networks [S1].
 
